From a4915142cd0a6ec3ea7616bfa516f7b5bab1bfe5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Volker Badziong <development@richtfest.tech>
Date: Fri, 17 Jul 2026 06:38:24 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 04/11] Manage per-server container scope properties via
 systemd D-Bus

With docker.per_server_slices.enabled, Wings applies each server's cgroup v2
resource properties -- memory.min/low/high/max floors and ceilings, cpu/io
weights -- directly to the docker-<id>.scope unit systemd creates for that
server's container, merging node-wide defaults with admin-only per-server
WINGS_CG_* overrides. Placement stays flat: containers are created under
docker.cgroup_parent itself, with no Wings-owned unit in between.

The scope is the leaf: the container's pages are charged to it, so a floor
set there is unconditionally effective. Setting the floor on an intermediate
parent slice instead only reaches the container when cgroup2 is mounted with
memory_recursiveprot, a host-wide flag a runtime remount can strip -- after
which every floor silently protects nothing while systemctl show and the
cgroupfs file both still report the configured number.

Properties go exclusively through SetUnitProperties(runtime=true), the
reload-safe systemd-owned channel; raw cgroupfs writes can be wiped by
daemon-reload. Wings never creates a unit here, which is what removes the
whole class of lifecycle work the parent-slice design needed: no
StartTransientUnit, no orphan garbage collection at boot, and no
transient-versus-administrator-owned discrimination, because there is no
longer a unit that systemd could auto-materialize ahead of Wings.

The one cost is timing. A scope does not exist until the container's init
process is launched, so the properties are applied immediately after
ContainerStart() rather than before ContainerCreate(). The container runs
unprotected for the few milliseconds in between; that window is bounded by
process startup rather than by workload and is accepted deliberately.

No budget arithmetic. Floors are applied exactly as configured, and an
over-committed tier is resolved by the kernel, which shares the parent
slice's protection among its children in proportion to each one's usage
below its own floor -- load-following, and no Wings code. What Wings keeps
is the tripwire: it sums the floors it has applied and compares them against
the tier slice's own MemoryMin, logging when they exceed it, because at that
point the configured floors have stopped being individual guarantees and
only the tier total still is.

Because the scope is the leaf, it is also where Docker writes the panel's
own container.Resources. A panel-side settings save on a running server
reaches Environment.InSituUpdate -> ContainerUpdate, and with the systemd
cgroup driver runc turns that into SetUnitProperties on the very same scope,
after Wings' own write. Left alone it silently replaces MemoryLow, MemoryMax,
CPUWeight and the BFQ weight with the panel's values -- including replacing a
deliberately tight WINGS_CG_MEMORY_MAX with a much looser panel memory limit.
InSituUpdate therefore re-asserts the scope properties once the Docker update
has returned. On the three-level shape this was unnecessary: the two writers
lived on different units and composed by the min-along-path rule. The
re-assert is deferred rather than tacked on at the end, so it also runs when
ContainerUpdate itself fails: Docker restores its own HostConfig on that path
but does not roll back the cgroup writes runc has already made, and a
half-applied update is the state that most needs repairing.

Properties are applied only to a container that is actually running. A
docker-<id>.scope lives exactly as long as the container's init process, so
for a container that exists but is stopped there is no unit to write to and
the D-Bus call fails with "Unit docker-<id>.scope not found". Both routine
paths hit this -- a settings save on a stopped server, and the pre-boot
Sync -> InSituUpdate that runs before the container is started at all -- and
without the guard each of them would log the "runs under the node tier
without its own resource guarantees" warning. That warning is the one
diagnostic this design leans on for a genuinely degraded server; making it
routine would train an administrator to ignore it.

The tripwire has one condition it cannot report by firing: a node whose tier
slice declares no MemoryMin of its own has nothing to compare the summed
floors against, so it can never fire at all. That is now said once per Wings
process, the first time a floor is applied against such a tier -- it cannot
be said at startup, because the tier's MemoryMin is only knowable over D-Bus
and configuration validation runs without a bus. A tier whose MemoryMin
could not be READ gets a separate line, and its own once-per-process budget:
the consequence for the tripwire is identical, but "the tier declares no
floor" is a statement about the administrator's configuration, and saying it
on the strength of a failed read asserts something that was never
established.

Property management is strictly best-effort: D-Bus failures degrade to plain
placement with a logged warning and never block a server. An explicit
WINGS_CGROUP_PARENT override remains supported and marks the server
hand-managed (only explicit WINGS_CG_* values applied, never node defaults,
and left out of the node's floor ledger); overriding with exactly the
node-wide value opts a server out entirely.

